body:
Aggregate<I> addresses several collections as one range. There is no streaming counterpart, and Read::chain is not one:

    a.chain(b)                  -> ["a1", "a2", "b1", "b2"]
    a_without_newline.chain(b)  -> ["a1", "a2",       "b2"]

b1 is gone and nothing reports it. Reader seeds a sentinel newline before byte 0 so a file opening with a delimiter needs no special case, and that sentinel is per stream rather than per file. Chained, the bytes do hold `TTTT>b1 x` on one line, so three records is the right reading of them. This is not a libsail bug, which is what makes it dangerous: nothing is positioned to notice.

The proposal is a reader over several sources that reframes at every boundary, presenting one global index space:

    let mut reader = Reader::over(paths, format)?;

    for n in Selection::new(reader.count()?, m, seed) {
        if !reader.advance_to(n)? { break; }
        take(reader.record());
    }

sail is already immune, because every multi-input command builds a fresh Reader per entry rather than chaining. That per-file loop is the behaviour to lift.
